<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Friedrich Karl vom Stein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Baron vom Stein, Karl vom Stein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Friedrich Karl vom und zum Stein was a Prussian statesman who, as chief minister in 1807–08, began the reforms that rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> after its defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. His October Edict of 1807 abolished hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, and his municipal ordinance of 1808 introduced town self-government. Dismissed under French pressure, he later advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> and helped organise the war of liberation against Napoleon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-friedrich-karl-vom-stein.jpg" alt="Heinrich Friedrich Karl vom Stein" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Christoph Rincklake · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portr%C3%A4t_des_Freiherrn_Karl_vom_und_zum_Stein_als_preu%C3%9Fischer_Minister.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Heinrich Friedrich Karl vom und zum Stein was born on 25 October 1757 on the family estate near Nassau, the ninth child of Karl Philipp Freiherr vom Stein and Henriette Karoline Langwerth von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a>, and came from an old Franconian family. Like many of his generation he admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>. In 1773 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> to study jurisprudence, and he also studied English history and politics. He then went to Wetzlar, the legal centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and on to Regensburg to observe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> early in 1780 and took service in the Prussian monarchy, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of mines and manufactures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In June 1785 Stein was sent as Prussian ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>. In November 1787 he was appointed director of the chamber of war and domains for the king's possessions west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. From 1796 to 1803 he was supreme president of the Westphalian chambers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, where he had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> canalised as an outlet for the region's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>. In 1804 he became minister of state for trade, abolishing restrictions on internal trade. In 1807 the king dismissed him as refractory and obstinate after he declined the foreign ministry, but after the Treaty of Tilsit Frederick William III called him back, on 8 October 1807, with very wide powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Stein was then for a time virtually dictator of a reduced, nearly bankrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The October Edict, issued at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> on 9 October 1807, abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> throughout Prussia from 8 October 1810, swept away distinctions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> between noble and peasant land and abolished class limits on occupations. On 19 November 1808 he issued a municipal ordinance giving local self-government to Prussian towns and to villages of more than 800 inhabitants. He also supported the military reforms associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a>, which made military service obligatory for all classes.</p><p>A letter of his hoping for a national rising like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s was seized by French agents, and in December 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> declared him an enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and confiscated his property. He resigned on 24 November 1808, fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 5 January 1809 and lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> until Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1812. In 1813 he administered East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>, where an assembly ordered a militia, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> he supervised the liberated territories. He died at Schloss Cappenberg on 29 June 1831. Research has shown that his credit for the reforms of 1807 and 1808 must be shared with Theodor von Schön and others.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinrich Friedrich Karl vom und zum Stein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1806 the reforms of <b>Karl vom Stein</b> and Karl August von Hardenberg created ministries, municipal self-government and a freer economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nassau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Cappenberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.32,7.8&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Nassau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4728 415 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Heinrich Friedrich Karl vom Stein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5178 685h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5178 685h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.32,7.8&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
